A||N||14||jaguiler||Black material in Engine Bay - it's like wool.. can we remove it?||||5||
Z||000000||jaguiler||04-03-2001||04:31 PM||||"yea i know Eric it looked like sh*t, i cleaned it up already. all that black material around the engine was a bi*ch to put out but i'm glad i did it cuz that crap keeps the heat in"<P>-That's a quote I got from another post.  It was kind of off topic so I did not post it there.  Anyway can we remove that black material?  I think it is really ugly and I would like to clean my engine soon  ...and I bet that stuff is real ugly when wet!  Is that material for fire prevention, for less noise?  or to protect the rest of the car from the heat of the engine bay?  Do we need it at all?  I have a stock 86 GT -- in the process of cleaning the engine and making some fixes.....  it is also my daily driver, so safety is a concern.<P>Thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||209.246.221.125||reg||5
Z||000001||Hartz||04-03-2001||04:33 PM||||You can remove it.  I believe it's primary purpose (other than looking terrible)is noise reduction.  That being said, neither of my cars have it, and I can't really notice much of a difference.<P>Enjoy,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||14
Z||000002||c m west||04-03-2001||04:38 PM||||every customer I have had buisness with always asks me to remove it. However I always leave the material on the firewall for sound deadening characteristics. All the rest does is trap heat and get fiberglass in your skin for a week - pitch it like theres no tommorrow, not to mention that in most cases the black finish underneath looks brand new when its off, think of it as GM wrapping your car for you so that the paint would be maintained for nearly 20 years - also I believe ( but not too sure ) that removing the material was a factory recall.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||jaguiler||04-03-2001||04:56 PM||||just the answers i wanted to hear...i guess everytime i open the back i will peel a little bit of it off....or is there and easy way to do it.... ie , wet, wd40? anything?<P>thanks<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||209.246.221.125||reg||6
Z||000004||c m west||04-03-2001||05:13 PM||||I usually use a pressure washer - cuts through it like a lazer - but however you choose - REMEBER GLOVES!!!! hehehe<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000005||N_Man||04-03-2001||06:44 PM||||Its super easy to tear out<P>USE gloves, Dust mask, and Safety glasses.<BR>The Black stuff makes the biggest dust cloud of stuff I don't wanna be breathing or getting in my eyes.<P>After take your car to a pressure washer and clean it up nice.||||reg||1
Z||000006||danny boy||04-03-2001||07:54 PM|||| I'll agree with the leaving it on the firewall. yank the rest out. wet it first or it will be a cloudy inhalation hazard mess.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||04-03-2001||09:30 PM||||I removed it entirely from an '86 2M4 and it is noticeably louder inside the car. ||||reg||7
Z||000008||Wade87GT||04-03-2001||10:03 PM||||If it's really nasty, use some hair spray on it. It'll cut down on the dust.||||reg||1
Z||000009||qwikgta||04-03-2001||10:38 PM||||I agree, please wear gloves, I was stupid and i did not, i was pulling slivers out of my hands for days<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>QWIKGTA<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||12
Z||000010||AkursedX||04-03-2001||10:53 PM||||I was going to ask this same question today.<P>I'm glad I read this before doing it because i would of just ripped away without gloves or anything.  At least i'll avoid some slivers now!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||04-03-2001||11:35 PM||||For those who haven't pulled the fiberglass out yet or have tried to change the water pump out on someone else's car with it. I found that getting some duct tape and just putting the sticky side down on your skin is the best way of getting out the slivers! The sticky stuff takes it (yeah and some hair) out but at least you don't suffer for days!||||reg||14
Z||000012||Formula88||04-04-2001||12:14 PM||||And when you wash your hands afterwards, use COLD water, not hot.  Hot water opens your pores and the stuff gets further in there, making it worse.  The cold will keep your pores closed making it easier to clean the stuff off.  I learned this installing fiberglass insulation for living... [ugh].  Thank god THOSE days are over!||||reg||1
Z||000013||jaguiler||04-04-2001||12:44 PM||||Imagine yourself naked rolling around in it, in the hot sun . . . <P>.....nevermind<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||209.246.221.125||reg||10
